## 3. Implementing Automated Evidence Collectors

Let’s write real-world automated checkers to demonstrate how engineers programmatically collect compliance evidence. We will focus on two foundational SOC 2 compliance requirements:
1. **Infrastructure Access Control**: Checking that all AWS IAM users have active Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA).
2. **Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC) Controls**: Ensuring that all pull requests merged to `main` have received at least one peer approval.

### Case 1: Programmatic AWS IAM MFA Auditor (TypeScript)

The following TypeScript code runs as a daily serverless function. It connects to the AWS IAM API, lists all console-active users, audits their MFA devices, and outputs an audit-ready compliance document.

```typescript
import { IAMClient, ListUsersCommand, ListMFADevicesCommand } from "@aws-sdk/client-iam";
import * as crypto from "crypto";

interface AuditReport {
  evidenceId: string;
  timestamp: string;
  controlId: string;
  system: string;
  status: "COMPLIANT" | "NON_COMPLIANT";
  details: Record<string, any>;
  signature: string;
}

const iamClient = new IAMClient({ region: "us-east-1" });

export async function auditIAMUsersMFA(): Promise<AuditReport> {
  const runTimestamp = new Date().toISOString();
  const evidenceId = crypto.randomUUID();

  const usersWithNoMFA: string[] = [];
  const auditedUsers: string[] = [];

  try {
    const listUsersResult = await iamClient.send(new ListUsersCommand({}));
    const users = listUsersResult.Users || [];

    for (const user of users) {
      if (!user.UserName) continue;
      auditedUsers.push(user.UserName);

      const mfaResult = await iamClient.send(
        new ListMFADevicesCommand({ UserName: user.UserName })
      );

      const hasMfa = mfaResult.MFADevices && mfaResult.MFADevices.length > 0;
      if (!hasMfa) {
        usersWithNoMFA.push(user.UserName);
      }
    }

    const status = usersWithNoMFA.length === 0 ? "COMPLIANT" : "NON_COMPLIANT";
    const details = {
      totalUsersAudited: auditedUsers.length,
      compliantUsersCount: auditedUsers.length - usersWithNoMFA.length,
      violators: usersWithNoMFA,
    };

    // Calculate cryptographic signature of payload to prevent tampering
    const payloadToSign = JSON.stringify({ evidenceId, runTimestamp, status, details });
    const signature = crypto
      .createHmac("sha256", process.env.COMPLIANCE_SIGNING_SECRET || "default-secret")
      .update(payloadToSign)
      .digest("hex");

    return {
      evidenceId,
      timestamp: runTimestamp,
      controlId: "SOC2-CC6.3", // Access Control - MFA
      system: "AWS-IAM",
      status,
      details,
      signature,
    };
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("Failed to run IAM MFA audit:", error);
    throw error;
  }
}
```

### Case 2: Git Peer Review Guardrails (Node.js)

To prove change management control compliance under **SOC 2** section CC9.2, we must verify that all production code is reviewed. The script below targets the GitHub REST API to confirm that the merge of a specific Pull Request was blocked until a peer approved it.

```typescript
import axios from "axios";
import * as crypto from "crypto";

interface PROptions {
  owner: string;
  repo: string;
  pullNumber: number;
}

export async function verifyGitHubPullRequestReview(options: PROptions) {
  const { owner, repo, pullNumber } = options;
  const GITHUB_TOKEN = process.env.GITHUB_PAT;
  const url = `https://api.github.com/repos/${owner}/${repo}/pulls/${pullNumber}/reviews`;

  try {
    const response = await axios.get(url, {
      headers: {
        Authorization: `token ${GITHUB_TOKEN}`,
        Accept: "application/vnd.github.v3+json",
      },
    });

    const reviews = response.data;
    // SOC 2 CC9.2 requires verifying that approvals occurred prior to code merge
    const approvals = reviews.filter((review: any) => review.state === "APPROVED");
    const isCompliant = approvals.length >= 1;

    const evidence = {
      evidenceId: crypto.randomUUID(),
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
      controlId: "SOC2-CC9.2", // Change Management
      system: "GitHub",
      status: isCompliant ? "COMPLIANT" : "NON_COMPLIANT",
      details: {
        repository: `${owner}/${repo}`,
        pullRequestNumber: pullNumber,
        approvers: approvals.map((app: any) => app.user.login),
        totalApprovals: approvals.length,
      },
    };

    return evidence;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(`Error auditing pull request reviews on PR #${pullNumber}:`, error);
    throw error;
  }
}
```

